st at three quarters of where we  were in 1974 so you have  greater assets chasing less and less  opportunities  uh there was a really good academic  paper published called the  u.s listing gap by folks out of i  believe cornell and ohio state  and they showed that back in 76 the the  market cap of u.s stocks was 3.3  trillion  which was you know 49 of gdp and today  it's 38 trillion which is  176 gdp right the average market cap of  a company was 650 million bucks back  then and today's 10 billion dollars  so yeah when you think about that it  naturally leads the price appreciation  because there's only so many places you  can allocate that capital  got it and so obviously um not only are  we seeing  um kind of this movement of capital but  this is all happening simultaneous to  a pandemic on economic recession kind of  all of the macro shifts that we've seen  how does um the uh kind of catalyst for  all of this  and then the reaction of lower interest  rates and a bunch of quantitative easing  how did that kind of perfect storm of um  events  change or accelerate kind of this shift  sure absolutely so you know what i've  said historically is  qe infinity kind of pushed people out  the risk curve right so it changed your  savings account into your checking  account the bond market into the savings  account the equity market the bond  market the venture markets the equity  market  and it gave rise to the crypto market as  the new venture market  right and you're kind of seeing that  happen so you know in in the 30 years  before the global financial crisis the  fed's balance sheet expanded by 700  billion dollars  that took 10 days in march right you  know we've injected seven trillion  dollars of liquidity into the system  year-to-date  uh with another trillion to two on the  way which is crazy when your bid ask is  now a trillion dollars  but it's not just the fed right so  cumulative central bank balance sheets  have exploded  so from 2000 to 2008 the balance shee